Same as ../roothostdarwin: this proves the VALUE of a compile-time constant
(`bit_rt_root_host_target`, 0 on x86_64-linux and 1 on aarch64-linux, selected by
an `asm` per-arch immediate). One call and one print, no heap object under test,
so there is nothing about rooting for a collector run to verify.

This program's OWN code never reaches a safepoint (no loop, confirmed by
inspection), but `BIT_GC=stress` still does not report zero. Measured on real
x86_64-linux hardware (`mustafa-desktop-wsl`, `bit` 0.4.1, 28 cores, via
`.claude/boxlock.sh run --need x86_64-linux`), 5/5 runs: `collections=31` at
the ambient worker count, `collections=31` pinned to `BIT_WORKERS=1`,
`collections=35` pinned to `BIT_WORKERS=4` — same worker-count-tracking floor
stress.bit documents (runtime/sched/workerrun.bit's `Worker.run`), just a
different absolute number on this arch/core-count/compiler-version than the
~101 measured on aarch64-macos. None of those fire at a back-edge this program
emitted, so they still verify nothing about this program's rooting.
